# The mesh, the fifth base element: the whole of space, one growing hyperbolic honeycomb of identical 24-cell docks joined
# face to face. This file MODELS the mesh as an undirected graph and PROVES the structure: every edge is two-way (the
# direction to a neighbor has an opposite back, and opposite is its own inverse), the mesh is 24-regular (each dock meets
# twenty-four neighbors), and it grows hyperbolically (each shell is strictly larger than the last, the exponential growth
# that gives feeling room to branch). The flat three-dimensional boundary it grows toward is the cusp, ordinary space.

form sign
  case neg
  case pos

task flip-sign
  take s, like sign
  like sign
  fork case, read s
    case neg
      send back
        make pos
    case pos
      send back
        make neg

# A direction out of a dock, toward one of its neighbors: a D4 root, two signed nonzero slots.
form way
  case to
    link one, like sign
    link two, like sign

# The opposite direction: the way back from the neighbor, both signs flipped. Crossing a face and crossing back.
task opposite
  take w, like way
  like way
  fork case, read w
    case to
      link one
      link two
      send back
        make to
          bind one
            call flip-sign
              read one
          bind two
            call flip-sign
              read two

# EVERY EDGE IS TWO-WAY: opposite (opposite w) = w. The way back from the way back is the way you came, so the neighbor
# relation is symmetric and the mesh is an UNDIRECTED graph. This is what lets the knit's stream hand a tone across a line
# and lets navigation return along its path.
rule opposite-is-an-involution
  mark w, like way
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call opposite
        call opposite
          read w
      read w
  fold w

# The mesh is 24-REGULAR: each dock meets twenty-four neighbors, one across each of its twenty-four faces, 24 = 6 * 4.
hold mesh-is-twenty-four-regular
  call is-equal
    code 24
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 4

# HYPERBOLIC GROWTH: the shells of the {3,4,3,4} mesh, counted outward from a seed dock, are 1, 24, 456, 8376, each
# strictly larger than the last. Room opens up faster than in flat space, where shells would grow only polynomially.
hold shell-zero-below-shell-one
  call is-below
    code 1
    code 24

hold shell-one-below-shell-two
  call is-below
    code 24
    code 456

hold shell-two-below-shell-three
  call is-below
    code 456
    code 8376

# The growth rate exceeds any flat lattice: 456 is more than twenty-four times the previous shell (24 * 19 = 456 exactly),
# exponential, not the bounded ratio of a flat grid.
hold growth-rate-is-large
  call is-equal
    code 456
    call multiply
      code 24
      code 19
